lity of backing out and this  contract would only be breached if the  misrepresentations had a material  adverse effect now the phrase material  adverse effect is a term of art used in  mergers and acquisitions law it's  usually defined in the contract in a  very long paragraph and here the  language has been interpreted several  times by delaware courts we'll talk more  about why delaware later on because  that's the whole thing in and of itself  but in delaware there is a high standard  for showing a material adverse effect  the burden of proof is usually on the  buyer usually material adverse effect is  invoked when the target company starts  having significant performance problems  but even if a company's performance is  in decline and is dramatic delaware  courts generally say that the time  period needs to be measured in years not  just a few bad months and as far as i  can tell delaware courts had never found  a misrepresentation to give rise to a  material adverse effect until 2018 in  the case called acorn versus fresenius  cobby ag the delaware court summarized  the buyer's burden of proof quote a  buyer faces a heavy burden when an  attempt to invoke a material adverse  effect clause in order to avoid its  obligations to close a short-term hiccup  in earnings should not suffice rather  the material adverse effect should be  material when viewed from the longer  term perspective of a reasonable  acquirer in the absence of evidence to  the contrary a corporate acquirer may be  assumed to be purchasing the target as  part of a long-term strategy the  important consideration therefore is  whether there has been an adverse change  in the target's business that is  consequential to the company's long-term  earnings power over a commercially  reasonable period which one would expect  to be measured in years rather than  months the buyer in acorn was able to  demonstrate that the magnitude of the  change in performance was material and  this poor performance last