nt instead of  five that's probably not material though  it's also probably important to note  that one reason that musk and his  lawyers have couched this as a failure  to provide info instead of focusing on  claims of misrepresentation is that the  misrepresentations are covered by the  material adverse effect standard whereas  the obligations of the company found in  the covenants  are generally considered material so if  they breached their obligations on the  covenants they have a better chance of  getting out of the deal itself  additionally musk's lawyers also claim  that twitter failed to run their  business in the ordinary course of  business by firing two key employees and  losing others in violation of section  6.1 of the merger agreement that section  of the contract requires twitter to seek  and obtain consent before deviating from  its obligations to conduct its business  in the ordinary course and preserve  substantially intact the material  components of its current business  organization and this is a very common  term you see in these kinds of  agreements the reason is obvious when  you're buying a company you want to make  sure that that company continues in more  or less the same vein than it started  and it doesn't completely change their  uh their business while you're in the  process of buying it and here twitter  fired its revenue product lead and the  general manager of consumer  and  other employees to it possibly because  elon musk was buying the company  so uh musk said that he objected to  twitter firing many talent acquisition  employees and that he doesn't like  twitter's hiring freeze quote the  company has not received parents consent  for changes in the conduct of its  business including for the specific  changes listed above the company's  actions therefore constitute a material  breach of section 6.1 of the merger  agreement now it is very rare for deals  to fall apart over these kinds of  obligations number one twitter legally  c