ed for over a  year this precedent probably isn't very  helpful to musk's claim that spam  accounts mean that twitter is  dramatically overstating his performance  in ways that could impact twitter's  profitability down the line in fact one  could argue that if there are uh more  bots than they know of then its  profitability can only go up even  further because he'll get rid of the  bots and make it even more profitable  per user  and this is especially true since musk  said he wanted to acquire twitter to  improve the product by eliminating the  bots the sec filings include voicemails  by musk where he presses twitter to get  the deal done quickly so he can fix the  spam accounts and make the company more  profitable musk then sent a voice  message saying quote if the deal doesn't  work given that i don't have confidence  in management nor do i believe i can  drive the necessary change in the public  market i would need to reconsider my  position as a shareholder this is not a  threat it's simply not a good investment  without the changes that need to be made  so even though musk was at the time the  company's top shareholder he didn't  think the stock was a good investment  unless changes were made he then offered  twitter a share price that was  significantly above its value in other  words elon musk made it clear from the  outset that his interest in twitter had  nothing to do with its current  profitability and he knew that there  were too many bots he said he wanted to  buy twitter to prove a point about free  speech and he said he wanted to make a  better product by authenticating users  eliminating spam and adding an edit  button so to sum it up it looks like  twitter has a pretty good argument that  they gave musk all the information that  he needed and was entitled to and  further twitter can certainly argue that  number one the bot numbers aren't wrong  but even if they were it's still not a  material adverse effect uh if the bot  numbers are seven percent instead of  five that's probably not material though  it's also probably important to note  that one reason that musk and his  lawyers have couched this as a failure  to provide info instead of focusing on  claims of misrepresentation is that the  misrepresentations are covered by the  material adverse effect standard whereas  the obligations of the company found in  the covenants  are generally considered material so if  they breached their obligations on the  covenants they have a better chance of  getting out of the deal itself  additionally musk's lawyers also claim  that twitter failed to run their  business in the ordinary course of  business by firing two key employees and  losing others in violation of section  6.1 of the merger agreement that section  of the contract requires twitter to seek  and obtain consent before deviating from  its obligations to conduct its business  in the ordinary course and preserve  substantially intact the material  components of its current business  organization and this is a very common  term you see in these kinds of  agreements the reason is obvious when  you're buying a company you want to make  sure that that company continues in more  or less the same vein than it started  and it doesn't completely change their  uh their business while you're in the  process of buying it and here twitter  fired its revenue product lead and the  general manager of consumer  and  other employees to it possibly because  elon musk was buying the company  so uh musk said that he objected to  twitter firing many talent acquisition  employees and that he doesn't like  twitter's hiring freeze quote the  company has not received parents consent  for changes in the conduct of its  business including for the specific  changes listed above the company's  actions therefore constitute a material  breach of section 6.1 of the merger  agreement now it is very rare for deals  to fall apart over these kinds of  obligations number one twitter legally  can't force anyone to stay  not since the 13th amendment was passed  and additionally  sometimes as a company you need to be  able to terminate people that happens in  the regular course of business so we'll  see if there's any hay here but it seems  unlikely so let's talk about what's  going to happen in the ensuing  litigation  nothing is certain in litigation but  based on the facts of this case in terms  of the legal arguments i'd much rather  be twitter than elon in this particular  situation now normally when you're  talking about a breach of contract and  contract damages you're talking about  expectation damages the party that  didn't breach is entitled to be where  they expected to be  after the contract was consummated and  you measure that by taking in this case  the promised price subtracting the  current price of the company and the  delta is the amount that would be  necessary to compensate you for where  you expected to be but here the parties  negotiated a bilateral one billion  dollar termination penalty they  considered that twitter might get a  better offer and might want to cancel  this deal so they could go with the  better offer and they also consider that  musk might just you know musk everything  up  but here this termination provision is  functioning like liquidated damages  provision it sets  the damages at one billion dollars now i  suspect that if elon musk could get out  of this deal by just paying a billion  dollars and walking away he would  probably take that deal and just walk  away but here's where things get really  really sticky because section 9.9 b of  the merger agreement gives twitter the  right to sue musk for specific  performance now that is a legal term of  art but that basically means that  twitter can sue musk and it can seek an  injunction from the court forcing musk  to close the deal now that is  particularly complicated here because we  are talking about a deal worth  tens of billions of dollars and it's  also complicated because the deal  requires outside financing from several  banks but the way that those agreements  are written the banks don't have a whole  lot of outs here now in law school they  teach students that specific performance  is very rare and it is courts generally  aren't in the habit of forcing you to do  something that you don't want see for  example the 13th amendment but it most  often happens with real estate because  it's thought that at least legally each  piece of real estate is a unique  thing that can't be compensated purely  by money but when you're dealing with  contracts courts would much rather make  people pay money and go their separate  ways however it happens more frequently  in the context of mergers and  acquisitions delaware courts have been  known to order specific performance in  just such a situation as this although  it's more common for buyers to try and  force specific performance dela reports  agree that sellers in the m a context  also have the right to force the sale to  completion  so this is what happened when a company  called ibp the nation's top beef  distributor entered into a merger  agreement with tyson foods the nation's  leading chicken distributor both  companies performed poorly after the  merger agreement was announced and  tyson's had a buyer's remorse it tried  to terminate the agreement but ivp sued  for specific performance and in  addressing whether a seller has the  right to sue for specific performance  the court found no quote compelling  reason why sellers and mergers and  acquisitions transactions should have  less of a right to demand specific  performance than buyers so the court  concluded that tyson had to go through  with the purchase the court said that  even figuring out the right measure of  cash damages would be too hard since the  amount was presumably huge the court  said that specific performance was  doable since during trial tysons  admitted that it just wanted a lower  price and that the acquisition was still  a good strategy for the company the  court also said that shareholders would  be better served on the whole by a  specific performance remedy rather than  a large damages award that did nothing  but cost tyson a large amount of money  and now i need to explain some trivia  about the delaware courts of chancery  where this case is going to end up  if you've been paying attention to  litigation you often hear people say  it'll take years to resolve anything and  that's usually the case that is not the  case in the delaware chancery courts the  delaware chance reports are basically  business courts on steroids they handle  mergers and acquisitions for breakfast  and delaware made a name for itself by  being the most business-friendly state  in the country and the delaware chance  records have a specific expertise  in dealing with these enormous business  disputes and they are fast this could  end up being resolved in months rather  than years and while the numbers are  very high the dollar amounts are very  high this is not a particularly complex  case uh in fact  elon musk is incredibly online so so  many of the statements that he has made  are  public and will absolutely be used  against him so we'll probably have an  answer to this question pretty quickly  and at base the litigation outcomes are  pretty stark  one billion dollars in damages or force  musk to pay 44 billion dollars to  acquire a company he doesn't want now in  reality depending on how litigation goes  i'm sure all of the parties will  probably negotiate something in between  maybe a bigger fee that lets elon walk  away uh or maybe a lower price and he  consummates the deal it's entirely  possible that everything was just  posturing for him to get a better deal  in the long term or maybe elon never  wanted twitter in the first place who  knows but the important thing is that  lawyers are going to get paid  and speaking of which there is the  breaking news that twitter has indeed  sued elon musk for one count of breach  of contract the company asked the  delaware quarter chancery to enjoy musk  from further breaches of contract to  compel him to fulfill his legal  obligations and to compel consummation  of the merger upon satisfaction of a few  of the outstanding conditions now maybe  it's just me but i find this particular  complaint to be a pretty hilarious  document you can effectively put  whatever you want into a complaint it's  your opportunity to tell your side of  the story and twitter is doing just that  but it also just savages elon musk using  his own statements and fills in quite a  bit of the story and it covers exactly  what you would expect that musk is in  material breach of the merger agreement  twitter alleges that musk didn't uphold  his obligations including financing  public disparagement of the company and  misuses information in the whole you  know backing out of the deal thing  but it also includes interesting tidbits  like musk didn't even read the  information and reports that twitter  gave him even though he's complaining  about not having enough information  and twitter alleges that he can't avail  himself of the 1 billion termination fee  and must specifically perform he must  consummate the deal for 44 billion but  given that this is elon musk this is  only the beginning of the craziness and  you'll be able to read all about the  future antics in today's sponsor morning  brew because that is my favorite way to  catch up with pop culture and the news  i've actually been subscribed to morning  brew for forever it's one of my absolute  favorite newsletters and i'm thrilled to  tell you why because if you didn't know  morning brew is a daily newsletter  delivered monday through sunday it gets  you up to fee on business finance tech  and law in just five minutes and it  provides a fantastic quick survey of the  most important issues while being witty  irreverent and informative it's really  really funny  and did i mention